claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:14:57 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> I'd like to have another tool that uses daemon(8) to daemonize it. It
>> should loop and do the work, and sleep if there are no inbox messages.
>
> This is posted as "[PATCH] src/lab-process-mail: Add a loop to run
> under daemon(8)" [1]. It adds lab-process-mail-loop, which runs

lab-process-maild - the convention for a tool like this is a d suffix.

and drop the src/ prefix for stuff like this, it's dir organization, not
useful information in the subject prefix.

> lab-process-mail, sleeps LAB_PROCESS_MAIL_INTERVAL seconds (300 by
> default), and runs it again.

60 second default

> Each run already fetches and drains the inbox, so the sleep only
> happens once the inbox is empty.

I think this needs to change. That's the tool trying to be a daemon. Now
that we have a daemon in place, it doesn't need an internal loop. It
looks for new input, processes it, and exits.

> The environment passes through, so LAB_BASE_BRANCH and NOTIFY_URL work
> as they do today. Start it with:
>
>     daemon -f -o ~/lab/process-mail.log -p ~/lab/process-mail.pid \
>         lab-process-mail-loop
>
> and stop it with kill $(cat ~/lab/process-mail.pid).

I'm not going to do all that.

This gets installed as a package, right? So I should be able to:

    lab-process-maild start

or stop of course.

Also I'm inclined to have it clear the env. So there should be a
~etc/lab-process-maild.conf that lets me set up the env... hopefully it
can do that.

I want to make the files to go ~/log/ and ~/var/run/ - not sure if
that's possible. That might need to be part of config. I think package
manifests have LOCALBASE or root base... though it would be cool if it
worked in both root and user cases without a config file.

> A failed run stops the loop. A stuck message is still the oldest in
> the inbox, so a retry would spend an agent pass on it every five
> minutes; lab-process-mail has already sent the "stuck" notice, and you
> restart the loop once it is dealt with. The cost is that a transient
> failure, such as a fetch during a network outage, also stops it, with
> no notice since lab-process-mail only notifies about stuck messages.
> If that bites, the fix I would propose is a distinct exit status for a
> stuck message, so the loop can retry everything else.

File a bug report to the list e.g. [BUG] lab-process-mail gets stuck

Describe the condition, diagnosis, and possible fixes.
